  "worked_solution": "Recall that $\\text{Null}(XY)$ contains all vectors that, when pre-multiplied by $XY,$ result in the zero-vector $0.$ \n\nWhen pre-multiplying a vector by $XY,$ we actually multiply each row of $XY$ by that vector. So, $\\text{Null}(XY)$ must be orthogonal to the row space of $XY,$ which is $\\text{Col}((XY)^{T})=\\text{Col}({Y}^{T}\\,{X}^{T}).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"\n\n**Note:** In general, the four fundamental subspaces of a matrix $A$ can be schematically visualized, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14093810/assets/de22dd5b352e1d5be48f4b6ed3b2a4657ae623c1bd288d3464559758ea334af7.png)",
